kei is the terminator for the abstractors (nu, ka, du'u si'o, etc...).  ku
is the terminator for gadri (lo, la, le, li).  Both can often times be
omitted, but sometimes not.  So you're right, they don't mean a whole lot.
 In fact, they don't carry any semantic information at all, they just serve
as syntactic closing braces.

(   lo(   nu(   klama lo( zarci  )[ku]     )kei     prenu  )ku melbi  )

(   the(    event-of(    going-to  a/the-store  )  type-of person ) is
beautiful )

